// Zombie: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== zombie.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Cane At Night
genre: zombie + historical-drama
tone: Humid, drum-haunted and furious
era: 1790 | period
setting: A sugar plantation on the northern plain of Saint-Domingue | fictional | farm
logline: A woman who buried her brother a month ago sees him cutting cane by moonlight in a gang that never stops, and learns what the planter's sorcerer has been selling: workers who cannot die and cannot run.
premise: On this island a person worked to death in the cane is replaced within the year. The one hope the enslaved hold is that death takes them home across the water. The story of the zonbi is the fear that even that can be stolen: a body kept back, without will, working. {celeste} is a washerwoman. Her brother {baptiste} died of fever at the mill. She saw him put in the ground.
synopsis: The first act is a rumour made flesh. A night gang works the far fields, silent, unfed, never whipped because it never slows. The overseer says they are hired from another estate. {celeste} follows the lantern and sees her brother's scar on a man who does not turn at his name. The second act is what a zonbi is here: not a monster that bites, but a person with the self taken out. The planter {de_lery}, modern, indebted and proud of his yields, has a partner in {maitre_simon}, a free man of colour who learned powders from a poisoner and sells labour that needs no food and fears nothing. The living fear the night gang and envy it. The old woman {manbo_rose}, who serves the spirits in the hills, tells {celeste} what is known: salt will wake them, and what wakes is grief, and rage, and then they will walk to their graves and lie down. The soul was not stolen; it was locked in a jar. {celeste} must choose between leaving her brother a tool and killing him properly. She steals salt from the big house, a pinch at a time, in a colony where salt is counted. In the third act it is August, and in the hills a ceremony is called that history will remember. {celeste} salts the night gang's gourd. Forty dead men stop cutting. They look at their hands. They do not turn on her; they turn, all together, toward the great house, and walk. What they do there they do slowly. Then each goes to the place he was buried and digs. {celeste} sits by her brother's grave until he is in it and says the name he was given at birth, which is not the one in the plantation book. Behind her the cane is burning on every horizon.
question: What is the worst thing slavery could take, and what happens when it is given back?
themes: the theft of death; will as the last possession; revolt as awakening
audience: Adults; viewers who want the zombie returned to its origin
viewpoint: With {celeste}; the dead are never seen from inside
stakes: Her brother's rest, forty souls, and a plantation on the eve of the only successful slave revolution
conflict: A sister's love against a system that has found a way to own people past the grave
ending: She wakes the dead with salt. They destroy the house and return to their graves. The plain is on fire.
experience: Dread without gore, an unbearable recognition scene, and a release that is terrible and just
look: Moonlit cane like a sea, lantern-lit faces without expression; red earth; fire at the edge of every frame
sound: Machetes in rhythm with no voices; drums from the hills that answer one another. Then silence when the cutting stops.
m: restore | system | survival-vs-humanity | pyrrhic
@ celeste | Celeste | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 28 | To give her brother back his death | She closed his eyes herself | Waking him means losing him again | Patient, watchful, capable of anything once decided | From mourner to the one who breaks the spell | Creole, low; sings to herself | Headwrap, lye-cracked hands, a twist of salt in her hem
@ baptiste | Baptiste | co-lead | none | undead | he/him | - | Nothing, until the salt | He was a carpenter and proud | His will is in a jar | Absent; then all grief | From tool to man to rest | Silent; one word at the end | Burial shirt, a scar through one eyebrow
@ de_lery | Armand de Lery | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 40 | Yields that clear his debts | He thinks of himself as enlightened | He has asked no questions about the night gang | Cultivated, humane in theory | From pride in his figures to a man watching his fields walk toward him | Polished French | Linen coat, a ledger, a glass of claret
@ maitre_simon | Simon Lafortune | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 50 | Money and safety among men who despise him | He is free and can never be safe | What he has made remembers him | Clever, bitter, afraid | From merchant of the dead to the first they look for | Smooth, bilingual | Good coat, a row of sealed jars
@ manbo_rose | Rose | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 70 | The dead honoured and the living ready | She remembers the other shore | She can tell the cure but not bear it for her | Stern, kind, unhurried | From keeper of knowledge to one who lights the fire | Proverbs, songs | White dress, a clay pipe, a rattle
~ celeste - baptiste | sister and brother | she buried him | she buries him again, by his own name
~ celeste - manbo_rose | seeker and elder | a question | a handful of salt blessed
~ de_lery - maitre_simon | planter and supplier | a profitable silence | abandoned to what they made
# The Night Gang | EXT | Far Cane Field | NIGHT | celeste, baptiste | See him | A lantern, forty men cutting without a sound. She says his name. The scarred man does not turn | {celeste} finds her brother among the dead | 6 | crowd=40, props=machetes+lantern, vfx=moonlit cane
# The Night Gang | INT | Great House, Office | DAY | de_lery, maitre_simon | Count the yield | Figures up a third. {de_lery} does not ask how. {maitre_simon} is paid in coin and looks at the jars on his own shelf | | 4 | props=ledger+sealed jars
# What Is Known | EXT | Hill Clearing | NIGHT | celeste, manbo_rose | Learn the cure | Salt wakes them. What wakes is grief. Then they go to their graves. {manbo_rose} asks if she is sure she wants him awake | | 6 | props=rattle+clay pipe
# What Is Known | INT | Great House, Kitchen | DAY | celeste, de_lery | Steal salt | A pinch from the cellar each day, under the eye of a household that weighs it. {de_lery} passes and compliments the linen | {celeste} commits to waking him | 4 | props=salt cellar
# The Salt | EXT | Far Cane Field | NIGHT | celeste, baptiste | Wake them | Drums in the hills. She salts the gourd. Forty men drink, and stop. They look at their hands. He looks at her | The dead wake | 7 | crowd=40, vfx=burning horizon, props=gourd+salt
# The Salt | EXT | Great House, Avenue | NIGHT | de_lery, maitre_simon, baptiste | Come to the house | They walk up the avenue slowly, together. {maitre_simon} runs first. {de_lery} stands on his steps with a glass in his hand | | 5 | crowd=40, stunts=house stormed, vfx=fire
# The Salt | EXT | Burial Ground | DAWN | celeste, baptiste, manbo_rose | Lay him down | He digs. He lies down. She says the name their mother gave him. On every side the plain is burning | | 5 | props=grave, vfx=burning plain
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the zonbi of Haitian tradition on the eve of 1791, with invented people
